A pool that goes looks for its objects among every object of every pool,
still claiming each step takes one away,
so a destroy costs what the whole machine holds, not what it destroys.
It forgets the same objects, so only the counting harness catches it.

Caught-by: fuzz-work
Report: a call took N paid steps in object and took away N

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -196,4 +196,7 @@
 {
-    for (struct obj_header *o = pool_first(pool); o != NULL; o = pool_next(pool, o)) {
+    for (struct obj_header *o = object_first(); o != NULL; o = object_next(o)) {
         LOOP_PAID(sched_forget_pool, object, "an object of the pool that goes");
+        if (o->pool != pool->base) {
+            continue;
+        }
         switch (o->type) {
